Mapping a Group Project to the GitHub Workflow
A Big History Group Presentation
1. Create Repository
Activity Part: Our group created a shared folder on Google Drive for the Big History presentation project.
Similarity: Both create a central, shared storage space where all project files will live.
Details: We set up the main folder, gave access to all group members, and uploaded the assignment instructions and our initial topic outline.
2. Create a Branch
Activity Part: Each group member was assigned a different part of the Big History presentation to research and prepare individually.
Similarity: Both involve creating an independent workspace to work on a specific task without affecting the main project.
Details: I was responsible for the "Origin of the Universe" section, while my partner worked on "Formation of Stars and Planets," and another member focused on "Emergence of Life."
3. Commit Changes
Activity Part: After finishing my research and slides, I saved them and uploaded them to our shared folder with a note.
Similarity: Both involve saving your progress and recording a specific milestone in the project.
Details: I named my file "Origin_of_Universe_Slides_v1" and left a message in our group chat saying my slides were ready for review.
4. Open a Pull Request
Activity Part: I formally asked the group leader to review my slides and consider adding them to the main presentation.
Similarity: Both involve proposing your changes to be merged into the main project and requesting a review from teammates.
Details: I messaged the group leader: "My slides on the Origin of the Universe are ready. Please check them and let me know if they can be added to the master presentation."
5. Discuss and Review
Activity Part: The group leader and other members reviewed my slides, pointed out missing scientific evidence, and suggested improvements.
Similarity: Both involve a peer-review process to ensure quality before the changes are accepted.
Details: The leader commented that I needed to add more evidence about the Big Bang and improve the slide design. I then revised my slides based on their feedback.
6. Merge
Activity Part: Once approved, the leader combined my slides with the other members' slides into one final presentation.
Similarity: Both involve integrating approved changes from a branch into the main project.
Details: The leader merged my "Origin of the Universe" slides with the "Formation of Stars and Planets" and "Emergence of Life" sections into one complete Big History presentation.
7. Deploy / Release
Activity Part: We presented the final Big History presentation to the class and submitted the file to the professor.
Similarity: Both involve delivering the final, completed product to the intended audience.
Details: We practiced the presentation together, checked the final slides for errors, presented in class, and uploaded the PDF version to the course platform before the deadline.
